   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.3. Increased capacity of member States to develop policies, legislation and other measures that are specifically aimed at facilitating the transition of enterprises to formality</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. The Zimbabwe Congress of Trade Unions-through the Zimbabwe Chamber of Informal Economy Associations is offering new and improved services to informal enterprises or own-account workers in Bulawayo (OK MART) with a view to facilitate their transition to formality: 
a. Over 200 operators were sensitised on Recommendation 204. 70 entrepreneurs have registered their companies to enable them to supply goods and services to local authorities and government after receiving Business Development Service Training using the Improve Your Business (IYB).  
b. In 2021 informal economy operators and their associations engaged in the national formalisation agenda through dialogue with other key stakeholders including local and national government departments.
c. In 2021 70 enterprises employing over 200 workers have accessed refurbished, OSH compliant workspaces under a Pilot Initiative with the Bulawayo City Council in partnership with a local private property management company-Old Mutual Zimbabwe, academia- National University of Science and Technology (NUST) and the Bulawayo SMEs Trust.

2. Based on rapid assessment of the effects of covid-19 on informal economy workers conducted in 2020- the Bulawayo Chambers of SMEs supported their membership to access their workspaces during lockdown restrictive measures.
a. In 2020, the Bulawayo Chambers of SMEs was authorised by Government to issue informal business operators and workers travelling passes to move between the residencies, workspaces, and to and from markets as well access service providers.
b. In 2020, 12 enterprises were registered and repurposed their businesses to respond to covid-19 and manufactured covid-19 PPEs (masks, sanitisers, hospital beds)  which they supplied to corporates including NUST and the National Social Security Authority(NSSA). 1a and 1b. In 2020-2021, the ILO through funding from ILO Regular Budget Supplementary Account resources organised the capacity building workshops and provided technical expertise.
1c.In 2021, the ILO extended grants through Regular Budget Supplementary Account and Regular Budget Technical Cooperation resources for workers and the informal economy association to engage with their membership on inputs to the national formalisation strategy.
1d. In 2020, the ILO facilitated a multi-stakeholder national survey for gaps and needs of informal economy workers that highlighted among others the need for reliable and safe workspaces.

2a.In 2020, the ILO engaged the Ministry of SMEs and advised the decentralisation of the issuance of the travelling passes which resulted in more informal enterprises and workers being able to be productive during the covid-19 lockdown measures. The ILO also supported the submission of a database of enterprises and workers to the Ministry for the purposes of registration and access to covid-19 financing. 
b.The 2020 Business Development Service Training using the Improve Your Business (IYB) training included new skills and innovation that resulted in operators re-purposing their sewing and dress making business to manufacture PPEs.</narrative>
   </description>
