  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.4. Increased capacity of constituents to provide adequate labour protection to workers in diverse forms of work arrangements, including on digital labour platforms, and in informal employment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Following a request by the government in 2020, in 2020-2021, a labour market diagnostic assessment (LDMA) integrating an informal economy diagnosis of enterprises and workers covering all sectors was developed, implemented and completed. The labour market diagnostic analysis (including recommendations) was validated by the government and social partners in May 2021 and published in September 2021.
The Diagnostic recommended the need to improve the quality of existing jobs by expanding the coverage of labour regulations and social insurance programmes to facilitate transitions from unemployment/inactivity into employment and from low to higher quality jobs.
This diagnostic supported the development of the national strategy on formalization (reported under 3.1.3). Throughout 2020-2021 the ILO supported the technical consultations and data collection and analysis on the LDMA leading to the validation of the report in May 2021. More specifically, the ILO provided technical support between August 2020 and April 2021 for the design and implementation of the diagnostic, including in designing the diagnostic tools, supporting the analysis of data and interpretation of results. The ILO reviewed and contributed to the drafting and finalization of the report and recommendations and covered the publication of 1000 copies of the findings of the diagnostic assessment. 

The ILO ensured a participative approach all over the process and the organization of the  stakeholders&apos; consultation and validation meetings that were conducted virtually. Meanwhile, the launch of the report, in October 8, was a hybrid meeting with 100 guest attending physically.</narrative>
   </description>
